            <ArticleTitle>The differential diagnosis of rheumatism; a clinical study of 500 cases.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>472-7</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Five hundred consecutive admissions to the rheumatism service of a large hospital were reviewed in an effort to establish criteria for accurate differential diagnosis in rheumatic disorders. Forty-one per cent of the patients presented evidence of articular involvement or arthritis; 28 per cent had non-articular rheumatism, embracing the various types of fibrositis; 7 per cent had musculoskeletal neuroses, and 24 per cent had a variety of diseases unrelated to the musculoskeletal system. A working classification of the various causes of musculoskeletal pain is presented and criteria for the differentiation of the individual diseases are suggested.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
